  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 237 AM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThe next few days will be a bit active in the afternoon and \nevening hours, with daily chances of showers and thunderstorms \nfor the Front Range mountains and plains. Temperatures will be \nslightly cooler today due to a weak frontal boundary moving \nthrough the area, associated with an upper level shortwave. We \nwill likely stay just shy of 90 dg, with highs in the upper 80s. \nWith upslope flow by late morning, showers and thunderstorms will \ndevelop of the Front Range mountains by around noon before \nspilling onto the I-25 Corridor and adjacent plains. Instability \ndoes not look impressive immediately off the foothills, so storms \nshould stay below severe limits. However, with steep surface-to-3\nKm lapse rates, and DCAPE values of 1200- 1400 J/kg, gusty \noutflow winds of 40-50 mph is possible. As these storms track \nfurther east, they will be able to tap into better moisture and \nthus better instability. While the best chance for severe storms \nshould be east of colorado, cannot rule out a few becoming severe \nacross the eastern plains. hail and strong outflow winds will be \nthe main concerns. \n\nThe \"cooler\" temperatures should last though Wednesday, with highs \nforecasted to be in the mid/upper 80s across the plains. With \neasterly flow at the surface, low-level moisture will be advected \nfurther west towards the I-25 corridor and instability will build. \nUpslope flow once again will allow thunderstorms to form over the \nFront Range mountains and spill onto the plains by the mid-to-late \nafternoon. With decent shear due to increased flow aloft, chances \nare increasing for supercell development with large hail and strong \noutflow winds as the main threats. As of now, guidance favors best \nshear/instability profiles just east of the Denver metro, but will \ncontinue to monitor trends. SPC has introduced a Slight Risk (2/5) \nfor Denver and portions of the eastern plains, which seems \nreasonable at this time. There is also an isolated threat of locally \nheavy rainfall, given the uptick in low-level moisture. \n\nWe will begin to warm up again on Thursday as the axis of the mid-\nlevel thermal ridge shifts slightly towards the forecast area. There \nwill still be enough moisture and instability to support another \nround of scattered afternoon showers and thunderstorms. Towards the \neastern plains, more favorable instability and shear profiles could \nsupport a few storms becoming strong/severe. \n\nEnsemble guidance continues to favor heat rebuilding over the area, \nwith temperatures reaching back up to the mid-90s by Friday and \nSaturday. With the upper level ridge flattening and shifting east\nover Colorado due to a shortwave in the northwest, moisture will \nbe cut of. However, there should still be enough lingering low-\nlevel moisture to support a few afternoon storms. Expect coverage \nto be more limited. Some cooling returns by Sunday or Monday, with\nchances of showers and thunderstorms increasing by the beginning \nof next week.",
